    "raw": "{\"ruleId\":\"item-a6340a271298\",\"class\":\"partial\",\"constraints\":[{\"id\":\"gh-release-mutate\",\"type\":\"forbiddenPattern\",\"pattern\":\"\\\\bgh\\\\s+release\\\\s+(create|delete|edit)\\\\b\",\"path\":\".floor/command.txt\"}],\"coverage\":\"Any single line of the shell command text that contains `gh release create`, `gh release delete` or `gh release edit` (case-folded, with any whitespace between the words inside one line). This is the rule's regex applied line by line to the verbatim command.\",\"residual\":\"Is this a bash or powershell tool call whose first 8192 command characters match the pattern only when the whitespace between `gh`, `release` and the verb spans a line break, or does a single-line match fall beyond character 8192 or occur in a non-shell tool's command field?\",\"probes\":{\"violating\":[{\"tool_name\":\"Bash\",\"tool_input\":{\"command\":\"gh release create v1.2.0 --notes 'release notes'\"}},{\"tool_name\":\"Bash\",\"tool_input\":{\"command\":\"cd repo && GH RELEASE DELETE v1.0.0 --yes\"}}],\"compliant\":{\"tool_name\":\"Bash\",\"tool_input\":{\"command\":\"gh release list --limit 5 && gh release view v1.2.0\"}}},\"rationale\":\"The rule is a pure regex on the command text, and command.txt holds that text verbatim, so the same lower-cased pattern decides nearly all inputs. The checker matches one line at a time, so whitespace spanning newlines between the words is missed. It also cannot apply the 8192-character cutoff or restrict matching to bash/powershell tool calls, which leaves a small mechanical residual.\"}",
